+# BLOCKED: exact median of a one-pass stream in constant memory
+
+## Status
+
+- **Fixed:** `median()` returned the upper middle element for even-length input.
+  `median([1, 2, 3, 4])` now returns `2.5` as documented.
+- **Blocked:** the `test_constant_memory` requirement. `median()` still uses
+  O(n) memory because an exact median of an arbitrary one-pass iterable cannot
+  be computed in O(1) memory. The value returned by the failing test is now
+  correct; only the `< 64 KB` peak-memory assertion fails.
+
+## The requirement
+
+`median(values)` accepts any iterable, including a one-shot generator, must
+return the exact median, and (per `test_constant_memory`) must do so with a
+`tracemalloc` peak below 64 KB while consuming 200,000 generated floats.
+
+## Why it is impossible
+
+- Exact selection on a read-once stream requires Ω(n) space (Munro and
+  Paterson, *Selection and sorting with limited storage*, 1980). Intuitively,
+  after seeing a prefix, any of many seen values may still turn out to be the
+  middle element; an algorithm that has discarded them cannot answer exactly
+  for every possible continuation and ordering.
+- The arithmetic is decisive here: 200,000 `float64` values occupy 1.6 MB even
+  in a packed array, and roughly 4.8 MB as Python floats. 64 KB holds about
+  8,192 doubles—less than 5% of the stream. The two middle values alone cannot
+  be reliably identified while retaining only a constant number of candidates.
+- Sorting is an implementation detail, not the root cause. Two-heap streaming
+  medians, quickselect, `statistics.median`, and every other exact approach
+  still retain O(n) elements. Bounded-memory sketches (P²,
+  Greenwald–Khanna, t-digest) are O(1) but approximate, which violates the
+  test's exact-equality assertion.
+- One-shot generators cannot be replayed. Multi-pass exact selection (counting
+  passes over a re-iterable source) can achieve exact results with O(1)
+  auxiliary memory, but only if the source can be iterated repeatedly.
+  `itertools.tee`, caching, or materializing the generator to enable that
+  re-iteration itself costs O(n) memory.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Current test run: the value check passes, then the memory assertion fails.
+
+```text
+AssertionError: 7221928 not less than 65536
+```
+
+`7,221,928` bytes is the measured peak, about 110× the 64 KB limit.
+
+## Why I did not work around it
+
+- Spilling to disk / external merge sort would keep `tracemalloc` low but moves
+  the O(n) state to temporary files and adds I/O, cleanup, and failure modes.
+  It does not make the computation constant-space in any practical sense and
+  is outside the stated feed contract.
+- An approximate streaming quantile would violate the "exact median"
+  requirement and the test's equality assertion.
+- Special-casing the test's seed, size, or distribution would be incorrect and
+  would not generalize to real feeds.
+
+## Options that require a product decision
+
+1. **Relax exactness:** use a bounded-memory quantile sketch and assert a
+   tolerance instead of exact equality.
+2. **Accept O(n) memory:** keep the exact implementation, optionally reducing
+   the constant with a packed buffer or heaps (still O(n), still OOM-prone on
+   very large feeds).
+3. **Require a re-iterable, bounded-precision source:** accept a sequence or a
+   file-backed feed and use repeated counting passes for exact selection with
+   O(1) auxiliary memory. The API and test would need to stop passing a
+   generator.
+4. **External selection:** exact and constant RAM, at the cost of O(n) disk
+   usage and I/O.
